A **cold reply** in a text or chat is a short, distant answer such as “ok” or “fine.” In sales, it means a prospect’s first response to an unsolicited email or LinkedIn message. A prospect can sound friendly and still be replying to cold outreach.

## Cold Reply in Chat: What It Means and How to Answer

A cold reply in chat is usually short, flat and closes the conversation instead of opening it. One cold reply rarely means much on its own; people answer briefly when they are busy or on their phone. A pattern of them over several days is the real signal. The best response is to match their length, ask one easy, open question, and give them space. If the replies stay cold, it is fair to ask directly whether now is a bad time, or to step back.

Cold email replies often read exactly like this too ("not interested", "send info"), which is why the rest of this guide covers how to answer them in sales.

## Cold Reply in Sales: The Meaning

In B2B outbound sales, a cold reply is a direct response from a prospect to an unsolicited cold email or LinkedIn message. It marks the shift from cold outreach into an active sales conversation. Unlike inbound inquiries where buyers request a demo upfront, a cold reply signals initial curiosity or objection, not a completed sale.

The "cold" refers to the relationship, not the tone. The prospect had no prior contact with you and never asked for your message, so a reply can be interested, a brush-off or a question. Compare it with a [cold email](/resources/glossary/cold-email), which is the message that triggers it, and track how often it happens with your [response rate](/resources/glossary/response-rate).

How fast and strategically your sales team responds in this first hour decides whether that curiosity converts into a qualified discovery call or goes completely cold.

## Cold Reply vs Warm Reply: What Changes

A warm reply comes from someone who already knows you or asked to hear from you, so you can move straight to a call. A cold reply comes from someone who owes you nothing. They are deciding in a few seconds whether you are worth another message, so keep the answer short, specific and easy to say yes to. For realistic reply-rate benchmarks, see the [Myntmore 2026 benchmark report](/resources/blogs/b2b-outbound-benchmark-report-2026).

## The Most Expensive Moment in Your Funnel

Think about what it took to get that reply: domains, warm-up, list building, copy, testing, weeks of sends. Every one of those costs lands on a single moment, when a real human types back. And that's exactly where most teams get lazy. They answer the next morning, paste a calendar link, or send a PDF deck nobody asked for. The reply rate looks great on the dashboard, but the meetings never show up.

## Speed Is the First Signal of Relevance

A well-known Harvard Business Review study of online sales leads found that most companies are not responding to leads nearly fast enough. A cold reply works the same way. When a prospect replies, they're thinking about your problem right then. Wait until tomorrow and you're back to competing with forty other tabs. Treat every positive or curious reply like an inbound lead, because that's what it just became.

## The Four Replies You'll Get (and How to Answer Each)

1. **"Send me more info."** This is rarely a yes. It's usually a polite way out. Don't send a deck. Send one sharp sentence and one question: "Happy to. So I send the right thing, is the bigger issue X or Y right now?" A real answer means real interest. Silence means you just saved yourself a wasted demo.
2. **"Not right now."** Ask when, not why: "Makes sense. Is it worth me checking back in Q1, or is this off the table entirely?" You get a date or a clean no. Both are useful.
3. **"Who else do you work with?"** They're asking for proof. Name one relevant client type and one specific outcome, then offer the call: "Mostly Series A SaaS teams stuck at the same stage you're in. Worth 15 minutes to see if it maps?"
4. **"We already have someone for this."** Don't argue. Get curious: "Good to hear. Out of interest, what would they need to be doing differently for you to look elsewhere?" Half the time, they'll tell you.

## Kill the Naked Calendar Link

Replying to a warm prospect with nothing but "Here's my Calendly" hands them the work and makes you look like one more automated sender. Suggest two specific times, add the link as a backup, and give one line on what they'll get from the call. The goal is to make saying yes easy, not to hand them admin.

## Reply Handling Is a System, Not a Mood

If replies sit in a shared inbox waiting for whoever gets to them first, your best leads are going cold. Assign ownership. Set a one-hour response window during working hours. Build a short library of answers for the four reply types above, and rewrite them every month based on what actually converts. Outbound doesn't end at the reply. That's where the real selling starts.
